# Line endings (SCRUM-315).
#
# Without this file, Git's `core.autocrlf=true` — the default on a Windows
# install — rewrites every text file to CRLF on checkout. Prettier's `endOfLine`
# default is `lf`, so `yarn check:format` then reports the entire repository as
# badly formatted: 229 files on the checkout that prompted this. The CI job
# passes regardless, because the GitHub runner checks out LF, which makes the
# check useless exactly where it is meant to be used — before pushing.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` normalises to LF in the index *and* in the working tree, on
# every platform, whatever the developer's `core.autocrlf` says. Setting
# Prettier's `endOfLine: "auto"` instead would silence the check rather than make
# the tree consistent, and would leave the shell scripts below still broken.
#
# The repository was already entirely LF when this landed — 283 text files at
# `i/lf w/lf`, zero CRLF in index or working tree — so this changes no content.
# It stops the tree from *becoming* inconsistent.
*                   text=auto eol=lf

# Executable scripts, where a CRLF is not a formatting opinion but a bug: the
# shebang line becomes `#!/bin/sh\r` and the kernel reports "no such file or
# directory" for an interpreter that is plainly there. Covered by the rule above,
# and stated explicitly because this is the case that fails loudest.
*.sh                text eol=lf
.husky/pre-commit   text eol=lf

# Binaries. `text=auto` already detects these by content, so this is a belt to
# that braces — but an explicit marker means a new image can never be mangled by
# a heuristic having an off day, and `-diff` keeps `git diff` from printing a
# screenful of bytes.
*.png               binary
*.jpg               binary
*.jpeg              binary
*.gif               binary
*.ico               binary
*.webp              binary
*.woff              binary
*.woff2             binary
*.ttf               binary
*.eot               binary
*.pdf               binary
*.zip               binary

# Generated, and reviewed as a whole or not at all. Still LF-normalised text —
# this only collapses it in diffs and in GitHub's language statistics.
yarn.lock           linguist-generated=true -diff
